Which test proves that $\Delta ABD \equiv \Delta CDB$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.95]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (C) at (4.3,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0.7,0);
\draw[tickmark2] (A) -- (B);
\draw[tickmark] (B) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark2] (D) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (D);
\draw (B) -- (D);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$AC = EC$ and $BC = DC$. Which test proves that $\Delta ABC \equiv \Delta EDC$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.95]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-2.4,0.7);
\coordinate (E) at (2.4,-0.7);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.8,-1.3);
\coordinate (D) at (1.8,1.3);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark] (C) -- (E);
\draw[tickmark2] (B) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark2] (C) -- (D);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (E) -- (D);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$O$ is the centre of the circle. Which test proves that $\Delta AOB \equiv \Delta DOC$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-1.732,1.0);
\coordinate (D) at (1.732,-1.0);
\coordinate (B) at (-0.174,1.992);
\coordinate (C) at (0.174,-1.992);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\draw (A) -- (D);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (D) -- (C);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below] at (C) {$C$};
\node[right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (O) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A proof that $\Delta ABC \equiv \Delta EDC$ has the line "$\angle ACB = \angle ECD$". Which reason justifies this line?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.95]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-2.3,0.8);
\coordinate (E) at (2.3,-0.8);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.9,-1.2);
\coordinate (D) at (1.9,1.2);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark] (C) -- (E);
\draw (B) -- (C) -- (D);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (E) -- (D);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A proof that $\Delta ABC \equiv \Delta ADC$ by RHS begins with "$\angle ABC = \angle ADC = 90^{\circ}$ (given)" and "$AB = AD$ (given)". Which statement correctly completes the proof?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (0.714,1.532);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0.714,-1.532);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (B);
\draw (B) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (D);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\coordinate (b1) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (b2) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (b1) -- ($(b1)+(b2)-(B)$) -- (b2);
\coordinate (e1) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (e2) at ($(D)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (e1) -- ($(e1)+(e2)-(D)$) -- (e2);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

It has been proven that $\Delta ABE \equiv \Delta CDE$, with the vertices written in matching order. Which statement must therefore be true?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=0.95]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}},tickmark2/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (-2pt,-4pt)--(-2pt,4pt);\draw (2pt,-4pt)--(2pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\coordinate (A) at (-2.2,0.9);
\coordinate (C) at (2.2,-0.9);
\coordinate (B) at (-1.7,-1.25);
\coordinate (D) at (1.7,1.25);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (E);
\draw[tickmark] (E) -- (C);
\draw[tickmark2] (B) -- (E);
\draw[tickmark2] (E) -- (D);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below left] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$PQ = PR$ and $PS \perp QR$. Which test proves that $\Delta PSQ \equiv \Delta PSR$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (P) at (0,3);
\coordinate (Q) at (-1.9,0);
\coordinate (R) at (1.9,0);
\coordinate (S) at (0,0);
\draw[tickmark] (P) -- (Q);
\draw[tickmark] (P) -- (R);
\draw (Q) -- (R);
\draw (P) -- (S);
\coordinate (s1) at ($(S)!0.3cm!(R)$);
\coordinate (s2) at ($(S)!0.3cm!(P)$);
\draw (s1) -- ($(s1)+(s2)-(S)$) -- (s2);
\node[above] at (P) {$P$};
\node[below left] at (Q) {$Q$};
\node[below right] at (R) {$R$};
\node[below] at (S) {$S$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$O$ is the centre of the circle and it has been proven that $\Delta OAD \equiv \Delta OBD$ (SSS), where $D$ lies on $AB$. Why does it follow that $OD \perp AB$?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\tikzset{tickmark/.style={postaction={decorate,decoration={markings,mark=at position 0.5 with {\draw (0pt,-4pt)--(0pt,4pt);}}}}}
\coordinate (O) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (A) at (-2,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2,0);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (3.124);
\fill (O) circle (1.5pt);
\draw (A) -- (B);
\draw (O) -- (A);
\draw (O) -- (B);
\draw (O) -- (D);
\draw[tickmark] (A) -- (D);
\draw[tickmark] (D) -- (B);
\node[above] at (O) {$O$};
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

It has been proven that $\Delta ABD \equiv \Delta CBD$, with $A$, $B$, $C$ on a straight line. Which conclusion correctly shows that $\Delta ACD$ is isosceles?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},scale=1.0]
\usetikzlibrary{calc}
\coordinate (A) at (-2,2.4);
\coordinate (B) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (C) at (2,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,0);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (B) -- (D);
\draw (A) -- (D);
\draw (C) -- (D);
\coordinate (b1) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (b2) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(D)$);
\draw (b1) -- ($(b1)+(b2)-(B)$) -- (b2);
\coordinate (b3) at ($(B)!0.3cm!(C)$);
\draw (b3) -- ($(b3)+(b2)-(B)$) -- (b2);
\node[above left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
